    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-000",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Definition of key terms",
      "pageStart": 7,
      "pageEnd": 8,
      "target": "../sections/sec-000-definition-of-key-terms.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Age groups\nThe following definitions for adults, adolescents, children and infants are used in these \nguidelines for the purpose of implementing recommendations for specific age groups. \nCountries may have other definitions under national laws.\n•\t An adult is a person older than 19 years of age.\n•\t An adolescent is a person 10–19 years of age inclusive.\n•\t A child is a person one year to younger than 10 years of age.\n•\t An infant is a child younger than one year of age.\nAdvanced HIV disease\nFor adults, adolescents and children five years or older, advanced HIV disease is defined \nas a CD4 cell",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines the age groups used in the cryptococcal disease guidelines and terms such as advanced HIV disease, cryptococcal antigen positivity, cryptococcal infection and disease, relapse, sustained clinical response and treatment failure.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-001",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Acknowledgements",
      "pageStart": 8,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-acknowledgements.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO gratefully acknowledges the contributions of many individuals and organizations to \nupdate these guidelines.\nGuideline Development Group\nCo-Chairs: Alexandra Calmy (Hôpitaux Universitaires de Genève, Switzerland) and Nelesh \nGovender (National Institute for Communicable Diseases and University of the Witwatersrand, \nSouth Africa).\nEduardo Arathoon (Hospital General San Juan de Dios, Guatemala), Mohamed Chakroun \n(Fattouma Bourguiba Teaching Hospital, Tunisia), Rachel Burke (Malawi Liverpool Wellcome \nClinical Research Programme and London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine, Malawi), \nTo",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Thanks the individuals and organizations who contributed to updating the cryptococcal disease guidelines, listing the Guideline Development Group, reviewers, WHO staff and regional offices. It notes that Unitaid funded the work.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Executive summary",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-executive-summary.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Cryptococcal disease is one of the most common opportunistic infections among people living \nwith advanced HIV disease and is a major contributor to illness, disability and mortality (1).\nBy far the most common presentation is cryptococcal meningitis, which caused an estimated \n223 100 incident cases and 181 100 deaths among people living with HIV in 2014 and \naccounted for 15% of all the people dying from HIV-related deaths globally (2). Cryptococcal \ndisease is uncommon among children with HIV.\nA public health approach leading to the prevention, earlier diagnosis and improved treatment \nof c",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarizes the burden of cryptococcal disease in advanced HIV, noting an estimated 181,100 deaths from cryptococcal meningitis in 2014, and explains that these guidelines replace the 2018 edition. It presents the main recommendations on diagnosis, antigen screening, preventive fluconazole and the new preferred induction regimen based on 2021 evidence. The summary also covers consolidation and maintenance treatment, deferred ART, managing raised intracranial pressure and steps for handling relapse.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-1-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Cryptococcal disease is one of the most important opportunistic infections among people living \nwith advanced HIV disease and is a major contributor to illness, disability and mortality (1–5). \nClinically significant invasive disease is primarily caused by reactivation of latent infection \namong immunocompromised individuals, such as people living with HIV, months to years after \ninitial exposure (6).\nBy far the most common presentation, representing up to 90% of HIV-related cryptococcal \ndisease, is cryptococcal meningitis, which caused an estimated 223 100 cases and 181 100 \ndeaths among peo",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the background to cryptococcal disease as an opportunistic infection in advanced HIV, its high mortality especially in low-income countries, and the 2021 trial that prompted the update. It notes that the disease is uncommon in children and mostly presents as meningitis.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Objectives and target audience",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-11-objectives-and-target-audience.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The objective of these guidelines is to provide updated, evidence-informed recommendations \nfor treating adults, adolescents and children living with HIV who have cryptococcal disease. \nThese guidelines are aimed at HIV programme managers, policy-makers, national treatment \nhealth care professionals and people living with HIV. \n2\nGuidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States that the guidelines aim to give updated recommendations on treating cryptococcal disease in people with HIV and are intended for programme managers, policy-makers, clinicians and people living with HIV.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guiding principles",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-12-guiding-principles.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The following principles have informed the development of these guidelines and should guide \nthe implementation of the recommendations.\n•\tThe guidelines are based on a public health approach to scaling up the use of antiretroviral \ndrugs along the continuum of HIV prevention, care and treatment.\n•\tRapid ART initiation regardless of CD4 cell count or immune status is the most important \npreventive strategy to reduce the incidence of opportunistic infections.\n•\tEarly diagnosis and prompt initiation of optimal antifungal treatment is essential to \nimproving survival and clinical and nervous syste",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the guiding principles behind the guidelines, such as rapid ART initiation, early diagnosis and antifungal treatment, and deferring ART after a positive antigen screen or confirmed meningitis. It adds that implementation should reflect local epidemiology and context.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Diagnosing cryptococcal disease",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-21-diagnosing-cryptococcal-disease.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The 2018 WHO recommendations for diagnosing cryptococcal disease remain unchanged (16).",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that WHO's 2018 recommendations on diagnosing cryptococcal disease remain unchanged.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-211-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Early diagnosis and treatment of cryptococcal meningitis is key to reducing mortality from \ncryptococcal disease. Health-care professionals should have a low threshold for suspecting \ncryptococcal meningitis among people with advanced HIV disease. National programmes  \nshould give priority to reliable access to rapid diagnostic cryptococcal antigen assays, \npreferably lateral flow assays, for use in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), serum, plasma or whole \nblood. In addition, health-care professionals need to have a low threshold for suspecting \ncryptococcal meningitis.\nDiagnosis of cryptococcal meni",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Stresses early diagnosis of cryptococcal meningitis and sets out the 2018 recommendation for prompt lumbar puncture with opening pressure measurement and rapid antigen testing. It describes diagnostic approaches according to access to lumbar puncture and rapid tests and lists contraindications to lumbar puncture.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0007\u0007RECOMMENDATIONS ON DIAGNOSING AND PREVENTING CRYPTOCOCCAL DISEASE",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-2-recommendations-on-diagnosing-and-preventing-c.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "PREVENTING CRYPTOCOCCAL DISEASE\n4\nGuidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV\nSummary of the diagnostic approach to cryptococcal meningitis\nLumbar puncture available\nLumbar puncture not available or \ncontraindicated\nRapid cryptococcal antigen test \navailable\nCSF cryptococcal antigen (preferably \nlateral flow assay)\nSerum, plasma or whole-blood \ncryptococcal antigen (preferably \nlateral flow assay), treat immediately \nand refer for further investigation\nNo rapid cryptococcal antigen test \navailable\nCSF India ink",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A summary chart of the diagnostic approach to cryptococcal meningitis, depending on whether lumbar puncture and rapid antigen tests are available.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rationale for the recommendations",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-212-rationale-for-the-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Rapid cryptococcal antigen assay in CSF, serum, plasma or whole blood (depending on access \nto lumbar puncture) is preferred based on the much higher diagnostic accuracy of these rapid \ncryptococcal antigen assays versus the India ink test and the fact that these rapid assays \ndepend less on the health-care provider’s skills. Advantages of the lateral-flow assay over the \nlatex agglutination assay include its rapid (<10 minutes) turnaround time, cost–effectiveness, \nminimal training requirements and laboratory infrastructure, no need for refrigerated storage \nand higher clinical and analytical",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that rapid cryptococcal antigen assays are preferred over India ink because they are more accurate and need less skill, and why lateral-flow assays are favoured. It notes that blood antigen testing does not replace lumbar puncture where feasible and that the adult recommendations are extended to children.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Preventing and screening for cryptococcal disease",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-22-preventing-and-screening-for-cryptococcal-dis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The 2018 WHO recommendations for prevention and screening for cryptococcal disease remain \nunchanged (16).",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that WHO's 2018 recommendations on preventing and screening for cryptococcal disease remain unchanged.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 21,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-221-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Early diagnosis and initiation of antiretroviral therapy remains the most important preventive \nstrategy to reduce the incidence of cryptococcal disease among people living with HIV and the \nassociated high mortality.2 Screening for cryptococcal antigenaemia is the optimal approach \nfor guiding resources in a public health approach and is the preferred approach for identifying \ninfection when managing people aged 10 years or older presenting with advanced HIV disease \n(20). There remains a role for fluconazole primary prophylaxis in settings in which cryptococcal \nantigen screening is not avai",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that early ART is the main way to prevent cryptococcal disease and sets out the 2018 recommendations on antigen screening before ART, pre-emptive antifungal therapy and fluconazole prophylaxis where screening is unavailable. It stresses evaluating everyone who screens positive for meningitis.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rationale for the recommendations",
      "pageStart": 21,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-222-rationale-for-the-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Since 2018, WHO guidelines have recommended that all adults and adolescents living with \nHIV who have a CD4 cell count <100 cells/mm3 be screened for cryptococcal antigen before \nART initiation or reinitiation; cryptococcal antigen screening may also be considered for \nadults and adolescents living with HIV who have a CD4 cell count <200 cells/mm3. These \nrecommendations were supported by evidence favouring the clinical benefit and cost–\neffectiveness of cryptococcal antigen screening (20,22,24–30). All individuals screening \npositive for cryptococcal antigen should be given pre-emptive antifu",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the rationale for screening adults and adolescents with low CD4 counts for cryptococcal antigen before ART and for pre-emptive fluconazole in those who test positive. It covers evaluating antigen-positive people for meningitis and when to stop primary fluconazole prophylaxis, citing trials.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Recommendations for children",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-223-recommendations-for-children.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "These recommendations apply to adults and adolescents with advanced HIV disease. The \ndecision not to extend these recommendations to children was based on the recognition that \ncryptococcal disease in this age group is rare, even in countries with high incidence (4,10).\nThese 2022 guidelines provide a new recommendation for the preferred antifungal regimen \nfor the induction phase of treatment.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the screening and prophylaxis recommendations are not extended to children because the disease is rare in them, and introduces the new 2022 induction recommendation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 23,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-31-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The 2018 WHO guidelines recommended seven days of amphotericin B deoxycholate and \nflucytosine-based therapy (followed by seven days of fluconazole 1200 mg) as the preferred \nantifungal regimen for the induction phase of treatment of HIV-associated cryptococcal \nmeningitis. This was a strong recommendation based on moderate-certainty evidence for \nadults and low-certainty evidence for children and adolescents (3). Liposomal amphotericin \nB is preferred over amphotericin B deoxycholate because of better tolerability. The guidelines \nrecommended the following as alternative options considering a",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the 2018 induction regimens for cryptococcal meningitis based on amphotericin B and flucytosine, along with alternatives when drugs are unavailable, and the extrapolation of evidence to children. It notes that fluconazole monotherapy is not recommended and that WHO met in 2022 to consider a single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B regimen.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0007\u0007RECOMMENDATIONS ON INDUCTION, CONSOLIDATION AND MAINTENANCE ANTIFUNGAL TREATMENT REGIMENS",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-3-recommendations-on-induction-consolidation-and.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "CONSOLIDATION AND MAINTENANCE \nANTIFUNGAL TREATMENT REGIMENS\n8\nGuidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A section heading for the recommendations on induction, consolidation and maintenance antifungal treatment regimens.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Induction therapy",
      "pageStart": 24,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-32-induction-therapy.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Induction therapy (2022 recommendations)\nA single high dose (10 mg/kg) of liposomal amphotericin B with 14 days of flucytosine (100 mg/kg per day divided into \nfour doses per day) and fluconazole (1200 mg/daily for adults; 12 mg/kg per day for children and adolescents up to a \nmaximum of 800 mg daily) should be used as the preferred induction regimen for treating people with cryptococcal \nmeningitis.  \nStrong recommendation; moderate-certainty evidence for adults and low-certainty evidence for children\nAlternative induction regimens\nIf liposomal amphotericin B is not available:  \nA seven-day c",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out the 2022 recommendation of a single high dose of liposomal amphotericin B with 14 days of flucytosine and fluconazole as preferred induction therapy for cryptococcal meningitis. It lists alternative regimens for when liposomal amphotericin B, any amphotericin or flucytosine is unavailable and stresses the value of flucytosine.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-017",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rationale for the recommendations",
      "pageStart": 24,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-017-321-rationale-for-the-recommendations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A systematic review from 2022 identified one randomized trial comparing a single high dose \nof liposomal amphotericin B, combined with oral flucytosine and fluconazole for 14 days, \ncompared with the preferred induction therapy recommended by WHO in 2018 (38).\nThe trial, conducted in Botswana, Malawi, South Africa, Uganda and Zimbabwe, enrolled 844 \nindividuals with HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis who were randomized 1:1 to either \nsingle, high-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg given with 14 days of flucytosine 100 \nmg/kg per day and fluconazole 1200 mg/day (liposomal amphotericin",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews the randomized trial in five African countries that compared single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B with the 2018 standard regimen in 844 people with cryptococcal meningitis. It reports that 10-week mortality was 24.8% versus 28.7%, showing non-inferiority, with fewer serious adverse events such as anaemia and kidney toxicity. The section also weighs costs, feasibility, acceptability and equity, noting the reduced burden on health workers.",
        "state": "draft",
