    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-023",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Adjunctive corticosteroids in treating HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis",
      "pageStart": 31,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-023-38-adjunctive-corticosteroids-in-treating-hiv-as.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "cryptococcal meningitis\nAdjunctive corticosteroids (2018 recommendation)\nRoutine use of adjunctive corticosteroid therapy during the induction phase is not recommended in treating adults, \nadolescents and children who have HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis.  \nStrong recommendation; high-certainty evidence for adults and adolescents and moderate-certainty evidence for children\n16\nGuidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV\nThe recommendation against the routine use of adjunctive corticosteroid therapy in the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Restates the 2018 recommendation against routine adjunctive corticosteroids during induction treatment of cryptococcal meningitis, citing trial evidence of more disability and adverse events. It notes that a paediatric advisory group found no reason for a different approach in children.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
