    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-025",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Rationale for the recommendation",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-025-391-rationale-for-the-recommendation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The optimal timing of ART after cryptococcal meningitis was informed by a systematic review \nthat identified four randomized controlled trials (62). Overall, early ART initiation increased \nmortality compared with delaying ART initiation (pooled relative risk for mortality at 6–12 \nmonths, 1.42, 95% CI 1.02–1.97). Immediately initiating ART is therefore not recommended \namong people with cryptococcal meningitis because of the potentially high risk of life-\nthreatening intracranial immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome.\nWHO strongly recommends deferring ART initiation for four weeks follo",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the evidence from four trials that early ART after cryptococcal meningitis increases mortality and specifies deferral periods depending on the induction regimen. It also discusses the limited evidence on ART timing for people who are antigen-positive without meningitis.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-026",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Implementation considerations",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-026-392-implementation-considerations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "ART-experienced people who develop cryptococcal meningitis should be evaluated for \npotential underlying ART treatment failure, ideally through confirmation by HIV viral load. \nART switches should be deferred by four weeks following an amphotericin B–based induction \nregimen or 4–6 weeks following a fluconazole-based induction regimen.",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Advises evaluating people already on ART who develop cryptococcal meningitis for treatment failure and deferring any ART switch by four to six weeks.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
