      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Raised intracranial pressure",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 35,
      "target": "../sections/sec-028-3101-raised-intracranial-pressure.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Initial measurement of intracranial pressure and management of raised intracranial pressure are \nan essential part of cryptococcal meningitis management to prevent death and serious nervous \nsystem complications. Raised intracranial pressure is a frequent and potentially life-threatening \ncomplication, occurring in up to 80% of people with HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis. \nThe limitations of using clinical symptoms or signs to identify people suspected of having raised \nintracranial pressure requiring repeat therapeutic lumbar puncture has been recognized (15).\nReduction of raised CSF p",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that raised intracranial pressure affects up to 80% of people with HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis and that lowering it improves survival. It recommends an initial and early repeat lumbar puncture with pressure measurement regardless of symptoms, with therapeutic drainage when pressure is high. Practical notes cover settings without manometers and the volume of fluid that can be removed.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
