    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-037",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Abstract",
      "pageStart": 53,
      "pageEnd": 54,
      "target": "../sections/sec-037-abstract.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Objective: The purpose of this systematic review is to provide updated evidence on the preferred \ninduction therapy for treating people with HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis, considering the \nmost recent evidence available, to inform the need for updates to WHO guidelines.\nDesign: Systematic review.\nMethods: We searched Medline via PubMed, EMBASE, the Cochrane Library and ClinicalTrials.\ngov for published or completed randomized clinical trials that evaluated induction treatment of \nfirst-episode HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis from 9 July 2018 (date of last search) through \n1 Sep",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The abstract of a systematic review on induction therapy for HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis, which found one trial of 844 people comparing a single high dose of liposomal amphotericin B plus flucytosine and fluconazole with seven days of amphotericin deoxycholate. It reports that the single-dose regimen was non-inferior for 10-week mortality and caused fewer severe adverse events.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-038",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 54,
      "pageEnd": 54,
      "target": "../sections/sec-038-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Cryptococcal meningitis is a leading cause of HIV-related mortality globally (1). A systematic review \nand network meta-analysis published in 2018 (2) provided evidence that, in resource-limited \nsettings, one-week intravenous amphotericin B deoxycholate and oral flucytosine induction therapy \nwas superior to other regimens that had been evaluated in clinical trials, and this formed the basis \nfor guidelines published by WHO (3). Despite this highly effective antifungal regimen, 10-week \nmortality in clinical trials is estimated to be about 35% (95% confidence interval (CI) 29–42%) and \nhigher",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the background to the review, noting that cryptococcal meningitis is a leading cause of HIV deaths and that even the 2018 WHO-recommended regimen leaves high mortality and toxicity. It cites a trial in five African countries showing a single high dose of liposomal amphotericin B with two oral antifungals to be as effective, which prompted this update.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-039",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Methods",
      "pageStart": 54,
      "pageEnd": 56,
      "target": "../sections/sec-039-methods.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Eligibility criteria\nWe included randomized controlled trials among people living with HIV with a first episode of \ncryptococcal meningitis evaluating induction regimens for treating people with cryptococcal \nmeningitis not currently recommended by WHO guidelines. The standard-of-care arm had to include \nan induction regimen recommended by the WHO guidelines, either one week of amphotericin \ndeoxycholate with flucytosine or two weeks of flucytosine plus fluconazole.\nWe excluded non-randomized studies. Additional exclusion criteria were laboratory or animal \nstudies, trials that did not evaluat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the review methods, including eligibility of randomized trials comparing new induction regimens with WHO-recommended ones in people with a first episode of HIV-associated cryptococcal meningitis. It lists outcomes such as 2- and 10-week mortality, fungal clearance and serious laboratory adverse events, and explains the statistical analysis and use of GRADE for certainty.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-357088/blocks/prose-sec-040",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Results",
      "pageStart": 56,
      "pageEnd": 57,
      "target": "../sections/sec-040-results.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Study selection\nWe identified 301 publications through database searches and one additional publication in press \narising from an abstract from other sources (Fig. 1). After de-duplication, 276 records were available \nfor reviewing titles and abstracts. Of these 276 records, 267 were excluded since they were not \nrandomized clinical trials involving humans with results available. Nine records were available for \nfull text review, of which eight were excluded; four did not use flucytosine in the comparator arm, \nthree had been previously included in the previous systematic review and one was co",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports the study selection, in which one trial of 844 adults in Botswana, Malawi, South Africa, Uganda and Zimbabwe met the criteria, and describes its non-inferiority design. It summarizes the risk of bias and GRADE assessments, rating certainty low for mortality and fungal clearance and moderate for severe adverse events.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
